00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:08.870 align:start position:0%
a disease is called rare if it affects  less than five in 10,000 people 30  million citizens in Europe are affected  by more than 6,000 different rare  diseases the information about these  patients is spread between hundreds of  registries across Europe at national  regional and local levels the problem is  that in most registries the number of  patients is too low to reach the  critical mass for scientific research  and studies health care for these  patients therefore suffers from  dispersed knowledge and fragmentation of  information about rare diseases to solve  these issues we need to work together at  a European level that is why the  European Commission has developed the  European platform on rare disease  registration the platform is composed of  the European rare disease registry  infrastructure and a data repository it  makes registries data searchable at EU  level and standardizes data collection  and data exchange  it will allow rare disease registries to  speak to each other in a common language  maximizing the value and the use of each  registry and its information now  researchers healthcare providers  patients and policymakers have a  consistent instrument to improve  knowledge diagnosis and treatment of  rare diseases  find out more about the European  platform on rare disease registration  [Music]
 

